# What F1 Lets Us Hear: A Study of Public Team Radio
**HCDE 410 Final Project: Preliminary Proposal**
Rebecca Liu · Autumn 2026

## 1. Motivation & problem

During a Formula 1 race, drivers talk to their teams constantly, but fans only hear a small selection of those conversations. F1 decides which clips are broadcast and released. These clips shape how fans see each driver: who seems calm, who seems angry, who seems funny, and who is barely heard at all.

I follow F1 closely, and I want to look at team radio as data. My research question is exploratory:

> **Which drivers, moments, and kinds of messages does F1 choose to make public, and how does this vary across races?**

Specifically, I want to explore:
- **Who** is heard: how released radio is distributed across drivers and teams, and whether it follows race position or team popularity.
- **When** they are heard: at what point in a race (pre-race, mid-race, final laps) and at what race position radio is released.
- **What** is said: what kinds of messages are released (strategy, car problems, complaints, emotional reactions, celebrations, team orders).
- **How much**: how the number of released messages varies by race and circuit.

This connects to a broader question I care about: how curated information shapes what audiences believe about people. The result will be an interactive visualization where users can explore these patterns and click on any message to hear it.

## 2. Data

**Source:** [OpenF1 API](https://openf1.org/docs), a free, open-source API with historical F1 timing data from 2023 onward. No API key is needed for historical data.

**What I collected:** Every team radio message available in OpenF1 for the 2023 and 2024 race sessions, joined with driver information and race position data from the same API. I have already pulled this dataset:

| Season | Races with radio | Radio messages |
|---|---|---|
| 2023 | 22 | 2,744 |
| 2024 | 24 | 2,620 |
| **Total** | **46** | **5,364** |

Each row is one radio message, with: season, circuit, timestamp, minutes from race start, driver name and number, team, race position at the time of the message, and a link to the audio clip (MP3, hosted on F1's live timing server). The 2023 Emilia Romagna GP has no data because the race was cancelled.

Two columns are derived by me: *minutes from race start* (message time minus session start time) and *position at radio* (the driver's most recent position record before the message).

**Planned processing:** Transcribe each clip with Whisper (open-source speech-to-text), then categorize message content. I will manually check a sample of transcripts and categories and report accuracy.

**Why it fits:** It is large enough for analysis (5,000+ rows), covers a full grid of drivers across two seasons, and combines structured data (time, position) with unstructured content (audio/text), which makes it good for exploration.

**License / terms:** OpenF1 is an unofficial community project and is not affiliated with Formula 1. It does not publish a clear data license, and the underlying data and audio come from F1, which owns the related trademarks and content. F1's [guidelines](https://www.formula1.com/en/toolbar/guidelines.html) say limited, non-commercial educational use may be acceptable. This project is non-commercial and educational. I will **not** redistribute the audio files in this repository; I will only link to them.

**Ethical considerations:**
- **Selection bias is the subject, not a flaw to hide.** The dataset only contains radio F1 chose to release, not every conversation. I will be clear that results describe F1's choices, not drivers' full behavior.
- **Real people, emotional moments.** Clips include frustration, swearing and stressful moments. I will avoid framing that ranks or mocks individual drivers (e.g., "angriest driver") without context.
- **Transcription errors.** Engine noise can cause Whisper to mishear words, which could put words in a driver's mouth. I will report accuracy, flag low-confidence transcripts, and always let users hear the original clip.
- **Biometric data avoided.** My original idea used driver heart rate data, which is not public and is sensitive health data. I chose not to pursue it.

## 3. Unknowns & dependencies

- **Audio access.** The clips are hosted on F1's servers, not OpenF1's. One source reports that F1 put its historical timing data behind an F1 TV subscription from October 15, 2026. I tested several clips from 2023–2025 on November 9 and they played, but access could change. **Fallback:** analyze timing and position only, without transcripts.
- **Shrinking coverage.** F1 released far fewer clips in 2025 (765 for the whole season) and almost none in most 2026 events. This is why I use 2023–2024.
- **OpenF1 availability.** OpenF1 is run by volunteers and limits requests to 30 per minute. I have already saved the dataset locally to reduce this risk.
- **Transcription time and quality.** Transcribing about 8–9 hours of audio depends on free GPU access (Google Colab), and accuracy on noisy radio is unknown until I test it.
- **Categorization effort.** Labeling 5,000+ messages by hand is not feasible. I will use semi-automated labeling plus manual checks, and may narrow the scope (e.g., a subset of races) if needed.

## 4. Feedback from classmates

A classmate pointed out that F1 heart rate data might not be publicly available. I confirmed this, so I switched from heart rate to team radio, which keeps my interest in what drivers experience during a race but uses data that is public.

## AI use

I used Claude (Anthropic) in three ways:
- **Finding data:** Claude helped me search for F1 data sources, confirm that heart rate data is not public, and identify OpenF1's team radio data.
- **Collecting data:** The raw data is not available as a single download. It is spread across four OpenF1 endpoints (sessions, team radio, drivers, position) and has to be requested race by race. Claude wrote and ran a script that collected these, joined them by driver and time, and exported one CSV (5,364 rows). Values come directly from the API; the only derived columns are minutes from race start and race position at the time of each message.
- **Writing:** Claude helped draft and edit this proposal. I reviewed and revised it.
